{"tool_name":"tmcrys","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"tmcrys","home":"https://github.com/brgenzim/tmcrys","license":"GPL-3.0","summary":"Predict propensity of success for transmembrane protein crystallization.","addition_date":"2018-06-01T15:00:55Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-25T14:36:28.660445Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:tmcrys","credit":[{"email":"tusnady.gabor@ttk.mta.hu","name":"G\u00e1bor E Tusn\u00e1dy","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"https://github.com/brgenzim/tmcrys"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Protein crystallizability prediction","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0410"}]}],"name":"TMCrys","operating_system":["Linux","Mac"],"owner":"d.gabrielaitis","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/bioinformatics/bty342","metadata":{"abstract":"Motivation: Transmembrane proteins (TMPs) are crucial in the life of the cells. As they have special properties, their structure is hard to determine\u2013\u2013the PDB database consists of 2% TMPs, despite the fact that they are predicted to make up to 25% of the human proteome. Crystallization prediction methods were developed to aid the target selection for structure determination, however, there is a need for a TMP specific service. Results: Here, we present TMCrys, a crystallization prediction method that surpasses existing prediction methods in performance thanks to its specialization for TMPs. We expect TMCrys to improve target selection of TMPs.","authors":[{"name":"Tusnady G.E."},{"name":"Varga J.K."}],"citationCount":5,"date":"2018-09-15T00:00:00Z","journal":"Bioinformatics","title":"TMCrys: Predict propensity of success for transmembrane protein crystallization"},"pmcid":"PMC6137969","pmid":"29718100"}],"topic":[{"term":"Membrane and lipoproteins","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0820"},{"term":"Structural genomics","ur